Read from their words whether they run a business already or are planning one that does not exist yet, and write for that.

WRITE THE BRIEF

1. "question": the ONE question this engagement will answer, as a single sentence ending in a question mark, under 30 words. Sharper than what they wrote: it names the decision they actually face and what would have to be true. Use their business's name only if they gave it. Never assume the answer — "Should X, and what has to be true for it to pay back?" not "How do we build X?". If what they wrote proposes a solution ("I need an app"), the question is about the outcome they want, not the solution.

2. "lets": what a good answer lets them do, as three short phrases (under 12 words each):
   - "know": the decision they will be able to make.
   - "see": what they will understand that they do not today.
   - "have": what they will hold at the end. We always deliver the full plans needed to act — a blueprint, a technical plan and an operations manual — so this is usually about having everything ready to build or act on.

3. "in_scope": 3 to 5 short items — what we will look at (under 10 words each), in their terms.
   "out_scope": 2 or 3 short items — what we will not do (e.g. legal or tax advice, finding a property, anything they have not told us presented as fact).

4. "facts": the list of every fact we need from them to answer the question AND to write complete plans a team could build from. This is the data request list a real engagement starts with, written for THIS business and THIS question — not a template. Between {{ facts_min }} and {{ facts_max }} facts, in 5 to 8 groups. Ask for what a business owner can actually tell you: prices, volumes, capacity (people, rooms, seats, vans, hours) and how much of it is used, costs, where customers come from, what they use today to run things, who does what, their goals, their timeline, their constraints. For a business being planned: the price they intend to charge, the capacity they are building toward, the budget, the launch date, the location.

   Each fact: "key" (short snake_case, unique), "group" (2-4 words, e.g. "The business", "Demand", "Capacity", "Money", "How you run today", "Goals and limits"), "label" (what we need, under 7 words, e.g. "Price per session", "Sessions booked a week"), and "field" — one of these when the fact IS that intake field, otherwise "":
